    <h1>Fall Real Estate Market Predictions 2026: San Antonio Edition</h1>
    <p class="lead">As we enter the fall season, the San Antonio real estate market is poised for interesting dynamics. Understanding these trends is crucial for military families making homeownership decisions in the final quarter of the year.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Interest Rate Trends: What to Expect</h2>
    <p>The Federal Reserve's recent decisions continue to influence mortgage rates. Industry experts predict that by fall 2026, we'll see stabilization in the 6-6.5% range for 30-year mortgages, with potential slight decreases if inflation continues trending downward. For military buyers using VA loans, this could mean significant savings compared to conventional financing.</p>
    <p>What does this mean for your budget? A 0.25% decrease in rates could save you thousands over the life of your loan. Monitor rate trends closely through September and October, as these months often present favorable windows for locking in rates before year-end buying pressure increases.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Inventory Levels: A Buyer's Market Taking Shape</h2>
    <p>Fall traditionally brings reduced home listings as families delay moving until spring. However, San Antonio's growing population and military influx continue to pressure inventory. We're projecting approximately 3-4 months of supply by September 2026, which is favorable for buyers compared to the previous spring's tight market.</p>
    <p>This balanced market means:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>More negotiating power for military buyers</li>
      <li>Lower average days-on-market as competition decreases</li>
      <li>Potential price reductions on homes lingering from summer</li>
      <li>Better selection in desirable military communities like Bulverde and Schertz</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Price Predictions by Neighborhood</h2>
    <p>San Antonio's real estate market isn't uniform. Different areas show distinct trends heading into fall:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>North Central (Bulverde area):</strong> Expect stabilization around $485-520K for quality homes. The military presence keeps demand steady despite seasonal slowdown.</li>
      <li><strong>Northeast (Schertz, Spring Branch):</strong> Prices likely in the $410-450K range for newer construction. This area continues attracting military families seeking value and modern amenities.</li>
      <li><strong>Medical District:</strong> Professional buyers maintain interest. Expect $520K+ for quality homes, with less seasonal volatility.</li>
      <li><strong>South San Antonio:</strong> More affordable options in the $300-380K range, attractive for junior military families and first-time buyers.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Military-Specific Market Dynamics</h2>
    <p>Military families make up a significant portion of San Antonio's real estate market. Fall 2026 brings specific considerations:</p>
    <p><strong>PCS Timing:</strong> Fall is typically quieter for military moves, but some families relocate mid-fiscal year. This means less competition from fellow military buyers, giving individual families more leverage in negotiations.</p>
    <p><strong>VA Loan Advantages:</strong> With strong inventory and reasonable rates, VA loans become increasingly attractive. Sellers know they're offering flexibility on terms, and many will accept VA offers more readily than during spring competition.</p>
    <p><strong>School Year Impact:</strong> Unlike summer, few families are willing to move mid-school year. This reduces competition but also means you'll have limited audience for any future sale timing on your new home.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>New Construction Trends</h2>
    <p>Builders in the San Antonio area continue active development, particularly in Northeast communities. Fall 2026 projections include:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Continued builder incentives to move inventory before year-end</li>
      <li>More attentive sales teams as competition for home sales increases</li>
      <li>Potential concessions on closing costs or upgrades</li>
      <li>Extended construction timelines for homes ordered in fall (delivery spring 2027)</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Military buyers considering new construction should understand that while current home prices may be favorable, homes ordered now won't deliver until spring 2027 at earliest, meaning timing considerations for PCS dates become critical.</p>
  </section>

